    "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Three-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition",
    "problem": "Express $\\frac{-{x}^{2}+20x+149}{(x+7)^{2}(x-3)}$ as a sum of partial     fractions.",
    "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+20x+149}{(x+7)^{2}(x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{x+7}+\\frac{B}{(x+7)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+7)^{2}(x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+20x+149=A(x+7)(x-3)+B(x-3)+C(x+7)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $x=-7$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-7)^{2}+20(-7)+149 & =A(-7+7)(-7-3)+B(-7-3)+C(-7+7)^{2} \\\\ -49-140+149 & =B(-10) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-40}{-10} \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(3)^{2}+20(3)+149 & =A(3+7)(3-3)+B(3-3)+C(3+7)^{2} \\\\ -9+60+149 & =C{(10)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{200}{100} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({0}^{2})+20(0)+149 & =A(0+7)(0-3)+B(0-3)+C(0+7)^{2} \\\\ 149 & =-21A-3B+49C \\\\ 149 & =-21A-3(4)+49(2) \\\\ 149 & =-21A+86 \\\\ 21A & =-63 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-{x}^{2}+20x+149}{(x+7)^{2}(x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{x+7}+\\frac{4}{(x+7)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{x-3}.\n$$",
